** — the ability to compensate for injury is diminished when baseline function is already compromised\n   - Chronic alcohol users have **worse TBI outcomes**: longer PTA, more severe cognitive impairment, slower recovery trajectory, and higher mortality\n   - Both conditions affect **overlapping neural systems** — frontal executive networks, hippocampal memory circuits, and cerebellar motor coordination\n   - Differentiating TBI-related cognitive deficits from alcohol-related deficits can be extremely difficult and requires serial neuropsychological testing over time\n\n3. **Relapse Risk**:\n   - TBI-related executive dysfunction and impulsivity dramatically increase relapse risk\n   - Patients with TBI + AUD have **relapse rates of 25-50%** within the first year after injury\n   - The combination is bidirectional: alcohol worsens TBI recovery, and TBI-related impulsivity worsens alcohol control',
