nk.\n\n3. **Nutritional Rehabilitation**:\n   - Continue **thiamine 100 mg daily** (lifelong in chronic alcohol users) — critical for preventing Wernicke-Korsakoff\n   - **Folic acid** 1 mg daily for macrocytic anemia\n   - **Nutritional supplementation**: High-calorie, high-protein diet. Albumin of 3.1 indicates nutritional depletion. Target BMI >22.\n   - Dietitian consultation for meal planning that supports both brain recovery and hepatic health\n\n4. **Rehabilitation-Integrated Substance Abuse Treatment**:\n   - **Psychology**: Weekly individual sessions using MI and CBT adapted for TBI\n   - **Group therapy**: Modified for cognitive accessibility — shorter sessions, visual aids, concrete examples, repetition\n   - **AA/12-step referral**: Introduce the concept but recognize that traditional AA may be challenging for TBI patients due to cognitive demands. Consider **brain injury-specific support groups** if available.\n   - **Education**: Provide TBI-adapted education about alcohol\'s effects on the healing brain using simple, concrete language with visual materials\n\n5. **Relapse Prevention Planning**:\n   - Identify triggers (stress, social situations, boredom, pain)\n   - Develop a concrete, written relapse prevention plan with the patient\n   - Involve the brother as a sober support person\n   - Plan for environmental modifications: remove alcohol from the home, avoid bars and drinking friends\n   - Establish a "crisis plan" with emergency numbers if craving becomes overwhelming',
