f he asks for one.\'\n\n*Turning to the brother*: \'[Brother\'s name], I know you are going to be his primary support at home. What are your thoughts about keeping the home alcohol-free during his recovery?\'\n\n**Key Communication Strategies Used**:\n- **Validated the mother\'s emotions** before delivering difficult information\n- **Used objective medical data** (BAL 0.28) rather than judgments to counter minimization\n- **Framed sobriety as medical treatment** rather than punishment or deprivation\n- **Addressed the genetic component** gently since the mother raised the father\'s alcoholism herself\n- **Avoided the label "alcoholic"** — used "alcohol use disorder" and "medical condition"\n- **Engaged the brother** as the practical ally for discharge planning\n- **Set a time frame** ("right now") rather than demanding permanent abstinence, which is less threatening and more achievable in the change process',
